WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.859
OpenAI and Cisco

00:00:01.671 --> 00:00:02.885
open-source Shirudo

00:00:02.744 --> 00:00:04.111
to shield unpatched

00:00:03.923 --> 00:00:04.996
Linux systems

00:00:05.526 --> 00:00:06.681
Shirudo is an

00:00:06.493 --> 00:00:07.766
open-source runtime

00:00:07.578 --> 00:00:08.698
that lets operators

00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:09.759
protect running Linux

00:00:09.571 --> 00:00:11.127
services while patches

00:00:10.939 --> 00:00:11.681
roll out

00:00:12.214 --> 00:00:13.452
Testing and deploying

00:00:13.264 --> 00:00:14.065
a patch can

00:00:13.877 --> 00:00:14.985
take days or

00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:16.034
weeks and blocking

00:00:15.846 --> 00:00:16.789
an exploit is

00:00:16.601 --> 00:00:17.426
hard when it

00:00:17.238 --> 00:00:18.204
relies on the

00:00:18.016 --> 00:00:19.619
same Linux operations

00:00:19.443 --> 00:00:20.952
as legitimate requests

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.286
Shirudo combines OpenAI's

00:00:23.097 --> 00:00:24.441
AI and security

00:00:24.253 --> 00:00:25.573
research with Cisco's

00:00:25.385 --> 00:00:27.000
infrastructure expertise

00:00:27.095 --> 00:00:27.991
and the companies

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:29.135
are inviting developers

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.503
security researchers

00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:31.387
and operators to

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:32.012
help build it

00:00:31.824 --> 00:00:32.319
out

00:00:32.838 --> 00:00:33.852
Full story on

00:00:33.663 --> 00:00:34.559
the dashboard
